WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.863
Welcome to your agent teamwork upgrade! No artificial intelligence doctorate required. Imagine

2
00:00:04.863 --> 00:00:09.725
a tiny support team: one person keeps the goal straight, another investigates,

3
00:00:09.725 --> 00:00:14.588
another builds, and another reviews. We will give Copilot similarly clear jobs,

4
00:00:14.588 --> 00:00:19.450
rather than asking a crowd of helpers to change everything at once.

5
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:24.297
Our practice app is an issue triage board with fictional support tickets.

6
00:00:24.297 --> 00:00:28.945
It is deliberately small. We will add priority, search, a summary, and

7
00:00:28.945 --> 00:00:33.592
an evidence export. The interesting part is how we direct the work,

8
00:00:33.592 --> 00:00:37.852
protect shared files, and check that the final result actually works.

9
00:00:38.052 --> 00:00:42.696
This is a visual lesson with an artificial intelligence generated voice, not

10
00:00:42.696 --> 00:00:47.340
a recording of live product clicks. Pause whenever you want to try

11
00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.984
an example. The live workshop still lasts three hours: one hour of

12
00:00:51.984 --> 00:00:55.854
presentation and demonstrations, followed by two hours of hands-on labs.

13
00:00:56.375 --> 00:01:01.022
Here is the whole workshop in three pieces. A custom agent is

14
00:01:01.022 --> 00:01:05.670
a reusable job description. A skill is a reusable recipe with supporting

15
00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:10.318
files. Your prompt is today's task: what to change, what to leave

16
00:01:10.318 --> 00:01:14.965
alone, and how to prove success. Different jobs need different tools, not

17
00:01:14.965 --> 00:01:15.740
bigger promises.

18
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:20.584
You will learn to give a helper a small, complete assignment. It

19
00:01:20.584 --> 00:01:25.229
needs the goal, the relevant context, the files it may edit, and

20
00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:29.874
the result to return. That result should contain evidence and unresolved questions.

21
00:01:29.874 --> 00:01:34.519
A confident sentence saying finished is not the same as a verified

22
00:01:34.519 --> 00:01:34.906
change.

23
00:01:35.106 --> 00:01:40.019
You will also learn why two chats can collide in one repository.

24
00:01:40.019 --> 00:01:44.932
Separate conversations do not guarantee separate files. We will compare clear ownership,

25
00:01:44.932 --> 00:01:49.845
a single integration writer, dependency ordering, and separate worktrees. The best choice

26
00:01:49.845 --> 00:01:54.349
is the simplest workflow that keeps the changes understandable and safe.

27
00:01:54.875 --> 00:01:59.532
The event clock is simple: sixty minutes together, then four labs of

28
00:01:59.532 --> 00:02:04.189
thirty, thirty-five, forty, and fifteen minutes. Demonstrations are inside that first hour,

29
00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:08.845
not extra time. Short check-ins are inside the labs. This self-paced video's

30
00:02:08.845 --> 00:02:12.338
running time is separate from the live event schedule.

31
00:02:12.538 --> 00:02:17.209
Complete the preflight before the workshop. Check Node, Git, your Copilot access,

32
00:02:17.209 --> 00:02:21.880
and the starter's tests. The app needs no database, secret keys, or

33
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:26.551
package installation. If an organization blocks a newer feature, use the documented

34
00:02:26.551 --> 00:02:31.221
fallback rather than bypassing the policy. Setup should not consume your lab

35
00:02:31.221 --> 00:02:31.611
time.

36
00:02:31.811 --> 00:02:37.011
Lab one creates useful instructions, an agent, and a skill. Lab two

37
00:02:37.011 --> 00:02:42.211
delegates research and implements priority. Lab three coordinates search and summary without

38
00:02:42.211 --> 00:02:47.412
overlapping writers. Lab four reviews and exports evidence. Each lab has prompts,

39
00:02:47.412 --> 00:02:52.612
checkpoints, hints, and separate instructor answers, so you can recover without guessing.

40
00:02:53.125 --> 00:02:57.771
A model proposes the next useful action. The harness is the control

41
00:02:57.771 --> 00:03:02.418
room around it: it supplies context, tools, permissions, and execution. A tool

42
00:03:02.418 --> 00:03:07.064
is an action such as reading a file or running a test.

43
00:03:07.064 --> 00:03:11.711
Changing the model does not automatically change the harness or its configuration

44
00:03:11.711 --> 00:03:12.098
format.

45
00:03:12.298 --> 00:03:16.943
A custom agent is a definition of a specialist. A subagent is

46
00:03:16.943 --> 00:03:21.588
a helper doing delegated work in a separate context. You can select

47
00:03:21.588 --> 00:03:26.233
a specialist yourself, or a main agent can call it for a

48
00:03:26.233 --> 00:03:30.878
side task. Built-in helpers can be subagents too; you do not need

49
00:03:30.878 --> 00:03:33.200
a new profile for every task.

50
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:38.045
Context means the notes available to the helper. A session is an

51
00:03:38.045 --> 00:03:42.690
ongoing unit of work with its conversation and workspace. Two notebooks can

52
00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:47.334
sit on the same desk. Likewise, two separate contexts can still edit

53
00:03:47.334 --> 00:03:51.979
the same checkout. Keep the job definition, the delegated call, and the

54
00:03:51.979 --> 00:03:54.301
actual workspace separate in your thinking.

55
00:03:54.833 --> 00:03:59.479
Here is our first collision. Two helpers have separate conversations, but both

56
00:03:59.479 --> 00:04:04.124
reach for the same file. One edit can overwrite the other. Subagents

57
00:04:04.124 --> 00:04:08.769
do not automatically get independent worktrees. A separate chat is useful for

58
00:04:08.769 --> 00:04:12.253
focus, but it is not evidence of filesystem isolation.

59
00:04:12.453 --> 00:04:17.592
Three protections answer three different questions. A worktree separates working copies. Tool

60
00:04:17.592 --> 00:04:22.731
permissions control actions that need approval. A sandbox can restrict access to

61
00:04:22.731 --> 00:04:27.870
files, networks, or credentials. None is a substitute for the others. A

62
00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:32.581
worktree is another workbench, not a locked room around your computer.

63
00:04:32.781 --> 00:04:37.427
Collisions are not limited to source code. Helpers in one checkout share

64
00:04:37.427 --> 00:04:42.073
its branch and Git index. Servers can fight over a port, and

65
00:04:42.073 --> 00:04:46.719
tests can overwrite a report directory. Before parallel writing, identify all the

66
00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:50.591
writable surfaces. If the boundaries are unclear, serialize the writes.

67
00:04:51.125 --> 00:04:55.772
The workshop's product facts were checked against official GitHub and VS Code

68
00:04:55.772 --> 00:05:00.418
documentation on November seventh, twenty twenty-six. That is a snapshot, not a

69
00:05:00.418 --> 00:05:05.065
promise that interfaces never change. Before teaching it later, check the current

70
00:05:05.065 --> 00:05:09.324
clients, policy settings, and file references rather than trusting old examples.

71
00:05:09.524 --> 00:05:14.516
The October twenty-eighth VS Code release documents Copilot as the default harness

72
00:05:14.516 --> 00:05:19.508
for new sessions. Older examples often assume Local. That matters: some frontmatter

73
00:05:19.508 --> 00:05:24.500
fields and delegation tools are different. Always choose the intended harness before

74
00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:27.828
copying configuration, and then inspect the runtime behavior.

75
00:05:28.028 --> 00:05:32.673
The Copilot app is documented for all Copilot plans, subject to organization

76
00:05:32.673 --> 00:05:37.318
policy. Its Projects experience is public preview. Marketplaces and connectors have preview

77
00:05:37.318 --> 00:05:41.964
limits, and computer use is experimental. Our core labs do not require

78
00:05:41.964 --> 00:05:46.609
these features, a cloud job, or external tools. Use the fallback when

79
00:05:46.609 --> 00:05:46.996
needed.

80
00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:52.145
The GitHub Copilot app is a desktop home for agent-driven development on

81
00:05:52.145 --> 00:05:56.791
Windows, macOS, and Linux. A Project can coordinate an ongoing goal; a

82
00:05:56.791 --> 00:06:01.436
session performs a bounded task. For this workshop, one repository and one

83
00:06:01.436 --> 00:06:06.082
small feature are enough. The diagram is an orientation sketch, not a

84
00:06:06.082 --> 00:06:07.630
screenshot of your build.

85
00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:12.475
When starting a session, choose the environment and workspace deliberately. New worktree

86
00:06:12.475 --> 00:06:17.120
gives a separate working copy. Current checkout uses the existing files and

87
00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:21.765
can overlap another writer. Read the selector and confirm the actual path.

88
00:06:21.765 --> 00:06:26.023
Do not assume the app's parallel sessions make every task independent.

89
00:06:26.223 --> 00:06:31.407
Plan lets you review an approach first. Interactive supports collaborative steering. Autopilot

90
00:06:31.407 --> 00:06:36.591
suits a clear task. Tool permissions are a separate choice: they decide

91
00:06:36.591 --> 00:06:41.776
which actions need approval. More autonomy does not remove your responsibility to

92
00:06:41.776 --> 00:06:45.664
inspect the changes and verify the outcome before integration.

93
00:06:46.167 --> 00:06:50.810
For the app demonstration, ask for a plan to add one triage

94
00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:55.453
behavior. Include the expected result, allowed files, and existing test commands. Ask

95
00:06:55.453 --> 00:07:00.097
it not to edit yet. The useful output is a bounded plan

96
00:07:00.097 --> 00:07:04.740
with affected paths and checks, not a proposal to rebuild the application.

97
00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:09.984
Review the plan. Reject surprise dependencies, databases, or unrelated improvements. Then allow

98
00:07:09.984 --> 00:07:15.027
the bounded implementation. Watch the changed files while it works. If it

99
00:07:15.027 --> 00:07:20.071
drifts, correct the same session with a concrete instruction. Confirm the active

100
00:07:20.071 --> 00:07:25.114
workspace before giving feedback; another conversation may be working on a different

101
00:07:25.114 --> 00:07:25.534
task.

102
00:07:25.734 --> 00:07:30.379
When it finishes, read the complete relevant diff and run the checks.

103
00:07:30.379 --> 00:07:35.024
Try the actual behavior in the board. An agent's completion message is

104
00:07:35.024 --> 00:07:39.668
not the acceptance criterion. If the desktop app is unavailable, do the

105
00:07:39.668 --> 00:07:43.152
same plan, implement, and review loop in VS Code.

106
00:07:43.667 --> 00:07:48.313
VS Code's Agents window puts agent work first, beside the normal editor.

107
00:07:48.313 --> 00:07:52.959
Open it with the command Chat: Open Agents window, or code, double

108
00:07:52.959 --> 00:07:57.606
dash agents. The sidebar organizes sessions and customizations, the center holds the

109
00:07:57.606 --> 00:08:02.252
conversation, and the review pane shows files and changes. Actual controls may

110
00:08:02.252 --> 00:08:03.801
vary with your build.

111
00:08:04.001 --> 00:08:08.746
Several panes can be visible, but only one is active. Select the

112
00:08:08.746 --> 00:08:13.490
intended session before feedback, terminal commands, or browser checks. Branch mode uses

113
00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:18.235
the selected current folder. New Worktree starts from committed files in a

114
00:08:18.235 --> 00:08:21.793
separate directory. Separate panes alone do not isolate edits.

115
00:08:21.993 --> 00:08:26.641
Supported sessions can continue between the editor and Agents window without starting

116
00:08:26.641 --> 00:08:31.289
over. Cloud and remote sessions have additional requirements; a remote host must

117
00:08:31.289 --> 00:08:35.936
remain available. We keep the core labs local, so you can see

118
00:08:35.936 --> 00:08:40.584
the paths, run the commands, and understand exactly where the changes go.

119
00:08:41.125 --> 00:08:46.167
Sharing an agent filename does not guarantee identical behavior across harnesses. Copilot

120
00:08:46.167 --> 00:08:51.208
uses native task delegation and supports a skills field. Local uses the

121
00:08:51.208 --> 00:08:56.250
run subagent tool and supports a named agents allowlist and handoffs. These

122
00:08:56.250 --> 00:09:00.871
are different contracts. Check both the schema and actual available tools.

123
00:09:01.071 --> 00:09:05.833
The target field tells the editor how to interpret the file. Target

124
00:09:05.833 --> 00:09:10.595
vscode means Local interpretation; target github-copilot means Copilot interpretation. It does not

125
00:09:10.595 --> 00:09:15.356
switch your session's harness. Select the harness in the interface first. A

126
00:09:15.356 --> 00:09:20.118
completion suggestion is helpful, but it does not prove the runtime supports

127
00:09:20.118 --> 00:09:20.911
that tool.

128
00:09:21.111 --> 00:09:25.756
Do not paste Local's agents list or handoffs into a Copilot example

129
00:09:25.756 --> 00:09:30.401
and expect enforcement. Do not paste Copilot's skills field into Local. The

130
00:09:30.401 --> 00:09:35.046
repository has separate examples. If native delegation is missing, run the same

131
00:09:35.046 --> 00:09:39.691
roles sequentially with a written handoff. The learning goal is reliable work,

132
00:09:39.691 --> 00:09:41.239
not a particular button.

133
00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:46.395
Make this smarter is a wish. Add this exact priority rule, preserve

134
00:09:46.395 --> 00:09:51.040
the issue shape, edit only this module and its tests, and run

135
00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:55.684
these checks is a task contract. The agent still chooses implementation details,

136
00:09:55.684 --> 00:09:59.555
but it no longer needs to invent your business requirements.

137
00:09:59.755 --> 00:10:04.400
Provide acceptance examples. One case should trigger the new behavior, one should

138
00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:09.045
remain unchanged, and one should test the exact boundary. Give the relevant

139
00:10:09.045 --> 00:10:13.691
files and existing commands. These examples are more useful than a long

140
00:10:13.691 --> 00:10:16.788
persona description or an instruction to be brilliant.

141
00:10:16.988 --> 00:10:21.633
Finish the brief with the expected report and a stopping condition. Return

142
00:10:21.633 --> 00:10:26.279
changed paths, exact checks, results, and remaining risks. Stop when the assigned

143
00:10:26.279 --> 00:10:30.925
behavior passes, or report a specific blocker. Do not expand into unrelated

144
00:10:30.925 --> 00:10:34.409
cleanup. Small, self-contained briefs make delegation easier to evaluate.

145
00:10:34.917 --> 00:10:40.025
Put durable repository rules in shared instructions: the runtime, test commands, fixture

146
00:10:40.025 --> 00:10:45.133
policy, and contract boundaries. The common Copilot file is dot github slash

147
00:10:45.133 --> 00:10:50.242
copilot instructions dot markdown. Path-specific instruction files can target selected areas. Keep

148
00:10:50.242 --> 00:10:54.924
them short enough to check and consistent with the task brief.

149
00:10:55.124 --> 00:11:00.480
Instruction formats and activation differ by surface. AGENTS dot markdown is supported

150
00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:05.835
in some experiences, not identically everywhere. GitHub documents that custom subagents in

151
00:11:05.835 --> 00:11:11.191
Copilot CLI do not receive repository instructions by default. Pass important invariants

152
00:11:11.191 --> 00:11:16.100
explicitly. Do not rely on invisible inheritance to protect shared files.

153
00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:20.946
Saying do not edit a file is guidance, not an operating-system lock.

154
00:11:20.946 --> 00:11:25.592
Combine instructions with appropriate tools, approvals, scope checks, and review. In lab

155
00:11:25.592 --> 00:11:30.238
one, you will write a small shared rule set and prove that

156
00:11:30.238 --> 00:11:34.109
your chosen agent and skill actually use the relevant guidance.

157
00:11:34.625 --> 00:11:39.270
Our first specialist is a reviewer. Its job is to find incorrect

158
00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:43.914
behavior and missing tests in the assigned files, then return actionable findings.

159
00:11:43.914 --> 00:11:48.559
It does not need to implement the fix. A focused role is

160
00:11:48.559 --> 00:11:53.204
easier to verify than a grand persona such as ultimate expert in

161
00:11:53.204 --> 00:11:53.591
everything.

162
00:11:53.791 --> 00:11:58.772
Give this reviewer reading and search tools, not editing or terminal execution.

163
00:11:58.772 --> 00:12:03.753
Define the report: file, problem, impact, and suggested fix or test. Check

164
00:12:03.753 --> 00:12:08.734
actual runtime activity because restrictions differ by harness. A read-only reviewer must

165
00:12:08.734 --> 00:12:13.716
not claim it ran tests; execution evidence comes from someone with execution

166
00:12:13.716 --> 00:12:14.131
tools.

167
00:12:14.331 --> 00:12:18.975
Start a fresh session with the intended harness and a small file

168
00:12:18.975 --> 00:12:23.620
you understand. Ask for a review without repeating the role instructions. Inspect

169
00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:28.265
the findings against the source and look at tool activity and workspace

170
00:12:28.265 --> 00:12:32.910
changes. Listed in the picker is discovery, not proof that the agent

171
00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:33.684
behaves correctly.

172
00:12:34.208 --> 00:12:38.852
A skill is a folder containing a procedure and any supporting resources.

173
00:12:38.852 --> 00:12:43.497
Our triage-policy skill has a SKILL dot markdown file, a policy reference,

174
00:12:43.497 --> 00:12:48.141
and a fixture inspection script. Copy the whole folder, not just the

175
00:12:48.141 --> 00:12:52.011
headline file. Otherwise the recipe can point at missing ingredients.

176
00:12:52.211 --> 00:12:56.856
The skill needs a name and description in frontmatter. The lowercase, hyphenated

177
00:12:56.856 --> 00:13:01.501
name must match the directory. The description explains what it does and

178
00:13:01.501 --> 00:13:06.146
when to use it. Refer to supporting files with paths relative to

179
00:13:06.146 --> 00:13:10.790
SKILL dot markdown. Keep the steps concrete and tied to the repository.

180
00:13:10.990 --> 00:13:15.636
Verify explicit invocation, then test a matching ordinary request separately. Automatic selection

181
00:13:15.636 --> 00:13:20.281
is useful, not guaranteed. Check whether the agent read the resource and

182
00:13:20.281 --> 00:13:24.927
used the right command. A skill does not grant tools or credentials.

183
00:13:24.927 --> 00:13:29.572
A reader-only role can describe a check, but cannot truthfully execute it.

184
00:13:30.083 --> 00:13:35.200
These are four different checkpoints. A skill can exist on disk without

185
00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:40.317
discovery. It can be discovered without selection. It can be selected without

186
00:13:40.317 --> 00:13:45.433
successfully running its script. Do not collapse the whole chain into a

187
00:13:45.433 --> 00:13:49.697
green installed badge. Inspect the actual activity and the result.

188
00:13:49.897 --> 00:13:54.543
Test a request that clearly matches the skill, one that should not

189
00:13:54.543 --> 00:13:59.190
use it, and one that is ambiguous. For triage policy, changing priority

190
00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:03.836
should load the policy. Explaining the page layout should not need to

191
00:14:03.836 --> 00:14:08.095
run priority checks. Refine the description when observed behavior is wrong.

192
00:14:08.295 --> 00:14:13.502
Review scripts and resources before using an acquired skill. A skill supplies

193
00:14:13.502 --> 00:14:18.709
a recipe; an MCP connection exposes external tools. Approve and review those

194
00:14:18.709 --> 00:14:23.916
tools separately. Marketplace availability is not a trust guarantee. The report should

195
00:14:23.916 --> 00:14:29.123
distinguish passed, failed, and not run. Missing capability must not become a

196
00:14:29.123 --> 00:14:29.991
success-shaped answer.

197
00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:35.144
Now combine the parts. The implementer is responsible for one change. The

198
00:14:35.144 --> 00:14:39.788
triage policy supplies a repeated procedure. The task brief supplies today's acceptance

199
00:14:39.788 --> 00:14:44.432
examples and allowed files. This is composition, not magic: each part has

200
00:14:44.432 --> 00:14:47.142
a clear responsibility that you can inspect.

201
00:14:47.342 --> 00:14:52.013
Copilot supports naming enabled skills in the agent's skills field. Use the

202
00:14:52.013 --> 00:14:56.685
exact skill name and confirm the inventory and actual loading. Preloading is

203
00:14:56.685 --> 00:15:01.356
not execution proof. The worker still needs appropriate tools and approvals, and

204
00:15:01.356 --> 00:15:04.471
the task still needs a narrow write set.

205
00:15:04.671 --> 00:15:09.396
Local does not accept that Copilot skills field. Use its supported discovery

206
00:15:09.396 --> 00:15:14.122
or explicit invocation instead. Do not give a researcher terminal access merely

207
00:15:14.122 --> 00:15:18.848
because an implementer uses a test procedure. Compose only what the role

208
00:15:18.848 --> 00:15:22.392
needs, then verify the behavior in the selected harness.

209
00:15:22.917 --> 00:15:27.840
In the live demonstration, create a workspace reviewer through Customizations, then inspect

210
00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:32.763
its file. Ask it to review a known small module. Look for

211
00:15:32.763 --> 00:15:37.687
useful findings and reading activity, not edits. If the report implies tests

212
00:15:37.687 --> 00:15:42.200
passed without execution, correct the role and repeat the small probe.

213
00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:47.260
Next, create the triage-policy skill with a real policy reference. Invoke it

214
00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:52.121
explicitly, inspect its resource use, then try a matching plain-language request. If

215
00:15:52.121 --> 00:15:56.981
automatic selection fails, refine the description rather than pretending installation guarantees use.

216
00:15:56.981 --> 00:15:59.817
Review the script before trusting its output.

217
00:16:00.017 --> 00:16:04.715
Finally, request a check that needs an unavailable tool. A good report

218
00:16:04.715 --> 00:16:09.413
says not run and explains why. It does not invent browser or

219
00:16:09.413 --> 00:16:14.111
test evidence. Lab one gives you time to create these files yourself

220
00:16:14.111 --> 00:16:18.026
and collect proof; the demonstration is inside the first hour.

221
00:16:18.542 --> 00:16:23.230
Use a subagent when a separate context contains meaningful independent work. Research

222
00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:27.918
a module, implement one owned feature, or review a completed change. Do

223
00:16:27.918 --> 00:16:32.607
not split a tiny edit into a crowd of helpers. Delegation adds

224
00:16:32.607 --> 00:16:36.513
model usage, waiting, and coordination; it should earn its place.

225
00:16:36.714 --> 00:16:41.513
An orchestrator keeps the overall goal, identifies prerequisites, assigns context and tools,

226
00:16:41.513 --> 00:16:46.312
then combines results. Fleet mode in the app or CLI can delegate

227
00:16:46.312 --> 00:16:51.111
suitable tasks, but it does not make unsafe writes safe. Start with

228
00:16:51.111 --> 00:16:55.910
one read-only specialist, then add parallelism only when work is genuinely independent.

229
00:16:56.110 --> 00:17:00.980
Local subagent calls are stateless and do not inherit the full conversation.

230
00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:05.850
A further Local review needs a new invocation with updated context. Copilot

231
00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:10.720
has its own delegation lifecycle. Do not teach one harness's behavior as

232
00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:14.373
universal. Always give the worker the important constraints explicitly.

233
00:17:14.875 --> 00:17:19.573
A research brief might say: locate triage behavior, read the contract and

234
00:17:19.573 --> 00:17:24.271
tests, do not edit or run commands, and return paths, patterns, and

235
00:17:24.271 --> 00:17:28.969
unknowns. Include the fictional-fixture rule and no-dependency constraint. The worker should not

236
00:17:28.969 --> 00:17:32.884
need to guess what happened earlier in your main conversation.

237
00:17:33.084 --> 00:17:37.898
For an implementation worker, replace read-only with the exact files it owns

238
00:17:37.898 --> 00:17:42.712
and acceptance examples. If it needs a shared contract change, it must

239
00:17:42.712 --> 00:17:47.527
stop and report that decision. Silent scope expansion is not helpful autonomy.

240
00:17:47.527 --> 00:17:52.341
The returned report should let the coordinator integrate without rereading every intermediate

241
00:17:52.341 --> 00:17:52.742
message.

242
00:17:52.942 --> 00:17:57.587
When a handoff is incomplete, identify the missing fact and supply it

243
00:17:57.587 --> 00:18:02.233
through the harness's supported lifecycle. For Local, start a new invocation with

244
00:18:02.233 --> 00:18:06.879
the relevant findings. Do not assume the earlier worker remembers a follow-up.

245
00:18:06.879 --> 00:18:09.976
A concise, self-contained brief is the portable fallback.

246
00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:15.784
Our conservative Local pattern has two readers and one writer. The researcher

247
00:18:15.784 --> 00:18:21.067
reports, the coordinator implements and validates, and the reviewer inspects. Readers cannot

248
00:18:21.067 --> 00:18:26.351
collide with implementation edits. This is a useful first multi-agent design for

249
00:18:26.351 --> 00:18:28.552
a small, tightly connected repository.

250
00:18:28.752 --> 00:18:33.397
Local's coordinator can list exact worker names in its agents field. Include

251
00:18:33.397 --> 00:18:38.042
the agent tool set. Picker visibility is a separate control: hiding a

252
00:18:38.042 --> 00:18:42.687
helper does not automatically prevent model invocation. The Copilot harness does not

253
00:18:42.687 --> 00:18:47.332
accept this Local allowlist field. Inspect runtime calls rather than relying on

254
00:18:47.332 --> 00:18:48.493
a copied example.

255
00:18:48.693 --> 00:18:53.873
The role describes a sequence, not a personality. Research first, implement within

256
00:18:53.873 --> 00:18:59.054
scope, validate, then request review. Fix actionable findings and provide updated context

257
00:18:59.054 --> 00:19:04.235
for another Local review invocation. At the checkpoint, inspect which files and

258
00:19:04.235 --> 00:19:07.257
tools actually changed, not only the frontmatter.

259
00:19:07.792 --> 00:19:12.736
Keep the same safe plan in Copilot, but use its native delegation

260
00:19:12.736 --> 00:19:17.680
and compatible fields. Pass the skill procedure, repository constraints, and expected result

261
00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:22.625
explicitly. Naming preferred workers in prose is guidance, not an equivalent Local

262
00:19:22.625 --> 00:19:25.921
schema allowlist. One owner still controls shared writes.

263
00:19:26.121 --> 00:19:30.766
The Copilot profile can use the agent tool alias and preload triage-policy.

264
00:19:30.766 --> 00:19:35.411
Leave out Local's agents and handoffs fields. The target helps the editor

265
00:19:35.411 --> 00:19:40.057
interpret the file; the session picker chooses the actual harness. Confirm enabled

266
00:19:40.057 --> 00:19:43.154
tools and skill use in the fresh session.

267
00:19:43.354 --> 00:19:48.223
Fleet can help split suitable work, but each helper consumes usage and

268
00:19:48.223 --> 00:19:53.093
separate context does not create a private filesystem. If delegation is unavailable,

269
00:19:53.093 --> 00:19:57.962
run the roles sequentially with written handoffs. Success is still the correct

270
00:19:57.962 --> 00:20:02.426
behavior, intended file changes, honest evidence, and no unapproved scope expansion.

271
00:20:02.958 --> 00:20:07.679
A handoff changes the active agent in your conversation. Local can offer

272
00:20:07.679 --> 00:20:12.399
a button with a prefilled implementation prompt after planning. This is useful

273
00:20:12.399 --> 00:20:17.119
when you want to review a stage before continuing. It is not

274
00:20:17.119 --> 00:20:19.873
parallel delegation, and custom-agent handoffs are Local-only.

275
00:20:20.073 --> 00:20:24.946
A subagent does a bounded side task and returns a result while

276
00:20:24.946 --> 00:20:29.819
the main agent keeps ownership. A separate session is independently tracked work

277
00:20:29.819 --> 00:20:34.692
with its own workspace choice. These are three different mechanisms. Choose a

278
00:20:34.692 --> 00:20:39.565
transition, delegated result, or independently tracked task based on your actual workflow.

279
00:20:39.765 --> 00:20:44.411
A missing handoff button should not block learning. Paste a concise reviewed

280
00:20:44.411 --> 00:20:49.057
plan, affected files, checks, and remaining decisions into the next role. If

281
00:20:49.057 --> 00:20:53.703
using a Local handoff, send false lets you review the prompt before

282
00:20:53.703 --> 00:20:57.575
submission. Preserve the decision point rather than automating past it.

283
00:20:58.083 --> 00:21:02.878
Let's make the priority example concrete. Active issues start at sixty points

284
00:21:02.878 --> 00:21:07.672
for high impact, thirty for medium, or ten for low. A blocked

285
00:21:07.672 --> 00:21:12.466
workflow adds twenty-five. At least one hundred affected users adds ten. At

286
00:21:12.466 --> 00:21:17.260
least seven days old adds five. Our first issue totals one hundred

287
00:21:17.260 --> 00:21:17.660
points.

288
00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:22.561
P one starts at eighty points, P two at forty, and lower

289
00:21:22.561 --> 00:21:27.263
scores are P three. This additive policy cannot produce exactly eighty, so

290
00:21:27.263 --> 00:21:31.964
test the nearest reachable scores: seventy-five is P two, eighty-five is P

291
00:21:31.964 --> 00:21:36.666
one. Also test ninety-nine versus one hundred users and six versus seven

292
00:21:36.666 --> 00:21:37.058
days.

293
00:21:37.258 --> 00:21:42.941
Closed issues override the bonuses: zero points, P three, and only the

294
00:21:42.941 --> 00:21:48.625
closed-issue reason. Research the contract, implement one owned module, run those cases,

295
00:21:48.625 --> 00:21:54.308
then request review. This demo proves a requirement, not merely that an

296
00:21:54.308 --> 00:21:58.571
agent wrote code. Lab two supplies the complete specification.

297
00:21:59.083 --> 00:22:04.022
Before parallel writing, spot the hot files: shared contracts, package manifests, lockfiles,

298
00:22:04.022 --> 00:22:08.961
server wiring, and common fixtures. If two tasks need one hot file,

299
00:22:08.961 --> 00:22:13.900
assign it to an integrator or finish that prerequisite first. Asking both

300
00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:17.604
workers to be careful is not a coordination plan.

301
00:22:17.804 --> 00:22:22.848
Also reserve ports, output directories, databases, and external actions. In one checkout,

302
00:22:22.848 --> 00:22:27.892
concurrent stage-all commits can scoop up another worker's unfinished files. Different branches

303
00:22:27.892 --> 00:22:32.936
in the same folder do not give separate workspaces. Our offline sample

304
00:22:32.936 --> 00:22:37.980
removes databases and credentials so the remaining boundaries are easy to see.

305
00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:42.923
If boundaries are uncertain, let readers investigate in parallel and keep a

306
00:22:42.923 --> 00:22:47.666
single implementation writer. That may feel less flashy than a swarm, but

307
00:22:47.666 --> 00:22:52.409
it is often faster overall because integration stays simple. Identify every writable

308
00:22:52.409 --> 00:22:56.362
surface, not only the source module named in the prompt.

309
00:22:56.875 --> 00:23:01.972
One reader can study priority while another studies the filter controls. Each

310
00:23:01.972 --> 00:23:07.069
returns findings; neither changes the shared checkout. The coordinator combines the reports

311
00:23:07.069 --> 00:23:12.166
and performs the edit. This keeps useful specialization without overlapping writers or

312
00:23:12.166 --> 00:23:13.440
competing Git commits.

313
00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:18.383
Workers can also return proposed patches without applying them. The integrator checks

314
00:23:18.383 --> 00:23:23.126
the current file version and applies deliberately. If the base changed, regenerate

315
00:23:23.126 --> 00:23:27.869
or rebase the proposal. Do not paste an old whole-file replacement over

316
00:23:27.869 --> 00:23:31.822
newer work. A patch still needs review and behavior checks.

317
00:23:32.022 --> 00:23:36.668
This pattern fits small repositories, coupled tasks, and newer teams. Two helpers

318
00:23:36.668 --> 00:23:41.315
examining one short file may only duplicate effort. Use separate contexts when

319
00:23:41.315 --> 00:23:45.961
their work is substantial enough to help. The advanced skill is choosing

320
00:23:45.961 --> 00:23:48.672
the workflow, not maximizing the agent count.

321
00:23:49.208 --> 00:23:53.854
A contract is the agreed input and output shape. Freeze that agreement

322
00:23:53.854 --> 00:23:58.499
before splitting work. For example, search and summary both consume the same

323
00:23:58.499 --> 00:24:03.145
issue shape. Neither changes it. File disjointness alone is not independence if

324
00:24:03.145 --> 00:24:06.242
one worker renames a field the other expects.

325
00:24:06.442 --> 00:24:11.087
Assign worker A the search module and its own tests, worker B

326
00:24:11.087 --> 00:24:15.732
the summary module and its tests, and the integrator the shared wiring

327
00:24:15.732 --> 00:24:20.377
and fixtures. Record acceptance checks and dependencies. If a worker needs another

328
00:24:20.377 --> 00:24:25.023
owner's file, it pauses and reports the decision rather than quietly expanding

329
00:24:25.023 --> 00:24:25.797
its territory.

330
00:24:25.997 --> 00:24:30.643
The ownership board is a team convention, not a filesystem lock. Run

331
00:24:30.643 --> 00:24:35.288
changed-path scope checks and inspect the combined diff. They can detect an

332
00:24:35.288 --> 00:24:39.934
out-of-scope edit, not magically prevent every write. Keep one owner for the

333
00:24:39.934 --> 00:24:44.193
board itself, so coordination notes do not become another shared-file collision.

334
00:24:44.708 --> 00:24:49.378
A dependency arrow means a prerequisite must finish first. If both features

335
00:24:49.378 --> 00:24:54.048
need the contract, agree and commit it before launching them. Then independent

336
00:24:54.048 --> 00:24:58.717
tasks can run together. List actual prerequisites; do not infer dependencies merely

337
00:24:58.717 --> 00:25:01.830
from the order tasks appear in a list.

338
00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:07.174
Manifest, lockfile, shared configuration, and board updates belong to a serialized stage.

339
00:25:07.174 --> 00:25:12.318
Let one integrator change them once. Workers report progress back instead of

340
00:25:12.318 --> 00:25:17.462
competing to edit the same coordination document. Reserve a single writer for

341
00:25:17.462 --> 00:25:20.462
staging and integration in a shared checkout.

342
00:25:20.662 --> 00:25:25.309
Two tasks can pass separately and fail together because their assumptions disagree.

343
00:25:25.309 --> 00:25:29.955
Retest the combined application and actual user flow after integration. If the

344
00:25:29.955 --> 00:25:34.601
dependency graph is mostly a chain, run it sequentially. Spend the saved

345
00:25:34.601 --> 00:25:38.472
attention on clear examples and review rather than artificial parallelism.

346
00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:43.739
An advisory claim is a sticky note saying who owns which paths,

347
00:25:43.739 --> 00:25:48.477
from which base revision, and until when. The coordinator checks conflicts and

348
00:25:48.477 --> 00:25:53.216
acknowledges the assignment before writing. At handoff, the worker reports its changed

349
00:25:53.216 --> 00:25:55.190
files and releases the claim.

350
00:25:55.390 --> 00:26:00.442
An expiry prevents a stale claim from blocking work forever. It does

351
00:26:00.442 --> 00:26:05.493
not authorize overwriting an active worker. Inspect the current state and resolve

352
00:26:05.493 --> 00:26:10.544
uncertainty. If a worker needs another path, request a new assignment. Markdown

353
00:26:10.544 --> 00:26:13.911
and chat claims are protocols, not operating-system mutexes.

354
00:26:14.111 --> 00:26:19.624
For many small projects, combine advisory ownership, appropriate tools, scope checks, and

355
00:26:19.624 --> 00:26:25.137
one integrator. Use worktrees when separate working copies help. Actual locking requires

356
00:26:25.137 --> 00:26:30.650
an enforcement mechanism with defined failure behavior. Do not market a task

357
00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:33.865
board as a built-in cross-agent security boundary.

358
00:26:34.375 --> 00:26:39.174
A scope check detects changed files outside an assignment. Required continuous integration

359
00:26:39.174 --> 00:26:43.973
can gate integration until tests pass. CODEOWNERS routes review to people; it

360
00:26:43.973 --> 00:26:48.773
does not lock local edits. These mechanisms are complementary. Be precise about

361
00:26:48.773 --> 00:26:52.772
whether a check prevents an action or detects it afterward.

362
00:26:52.972 --> 00:26:57.863
Give each role only needed capabilities, then inspect actual activity. Terminal execution

363
00:26:57.863 --> 00:27:02.753
is broad: a shell can write files even without a separate edit

364
00:27:02.753 --> 00:27:07.644
tool. A do-not-edit instruction alone is not a permission boundary. Keep approvals

365
00:27:07.644 --> 00:27:10.905
and machine access separate from repository ownership conventions.

366
00:27:11.105 --> 00:27:16.009
Hooks can run checks before or after tools. VS Code's hooks experience

367
00:27:16.009 --> 00:27:20.914
is preview and harness formats differ. A post-tool hook cannot undo a

368
00:27:20.914 --> 00:27:25.818
completed action. Copilot command pre-tool errors deny, but timeouts fall through to

369
00:27:25.818 --> 00:27:30.723
normal permissions. Test failure behavior; our core labs do not need hooks.

370
00:27:31.250 --> 00:27:36.834
A Git worktree provides another working directory, normally on another branch. Independent

371
00:27:36.834 --> 00:27:42.419
workers edit separate copies. Start from an agreed committed baseline containing the

372
00:27:42.419 --> 00:27:48.003
contract and customizations. Uncommitted agent files, ignored dependencies, and local configuration do

373
00:27:48.003 --> 00:27:51.260
not automatically appear in a new worktree.

374
00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:56.650
Worktrees separate working files and indices, not every part of Git or

375
00:27:56.650 --> 00:28:01.839
your machine. Give servers different ports and outputs separate locations. They do

376
00:28:01.839 --> 00:28:07.029
not protect credentials or external services by themselves. Sandboxing and permission controls

377
00:28:07.029 --> 00:28:08.326
address different risks.

378
00:28:08.526 --> 00:28:13.173
A task commit remains on its task branch until you integrate it.

379
00:28:13.173 --> 00:28:17.819
Review the scope and evidence, then use your normal merge, cherry-pick, or

380
00:28:17.819 --> 00:28:22.466
pull-request workflow. Keep the worktree until integration is verified. Do not delete

381
00:28:22.466 --> 00:28:25.951
useful work just because the last response sounded successful.

382
00:28:26.458 --> 00:28:31.447
Integration is a separate stage with one owner. Inspect each task's diff,

383
00:28:31.447 --> 00:28:36.436
scope, and test evidence. Integrate one task at a time. If shared

384
00:28:36.436 --> 00:28:41.424
assumptions changed, resolve them against the agreed contract before accepting another worker's

385
00:28:41.424 --> 00:28:41.840
result.

386
00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:46.684
A conflict requires reasoning. Read both intentions and preserve the required behavior;

387
00:28:46.684 --> 00:28:51.328
do not blindly choose ours or theirs. Even a clean merge can

388
00:28:51.328 --> 00:28:55.972
be wrong if the features disagree on fields, filtering, or order. Combining

389
00:28:55.972 --> 00:28:59.455
text is not the same as combining correct behavior.

390
00:28:59.655 --> 00:29:04.301
Run the relevant combined checks and try the user flow. In our

391
00:29:04.301 --> 00:29:08.947
board, search, priority, summary, and export must agree on the visible issues.

392
00:29:08.947 --> 00:29:13.593
Document remaining risks honestly. A useful integration handoff is more than a

393
00:29:13.593 --> 00:29:17.465
merge commit or a collection of separate green test messages.

394
00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:22.833
The third demo compares shared-checkout readers with one writer, and committed contracts

395
00:29:22.833 --> 00:29:27.666
with separate worktrees. Point out the actual paths and owners, not just

396
00:29:27.666 --> 00:29:32.500
separate tabs. Demonstrate a harmless scope violation caught before integration, then correct

397
00:29:32.500 --> 00:29:35.722
it rather than accepting an expanded write set.

398
00:29:35.922 --> 00:29:40.862
Now try the combined example. Searching uppercase PAYMENTS finds issues one hundred

399
00:29:40.862 --> 00:29:45.803
and one and one hundred and five. Search includes the closed issue

400
00:29:45.803 --> 00:29:50.743
because it matches the category. The visible summary is two total, one

401
00:29:50.743 --> 00:29:53.214
active, and one active blocked issue.

402
00:29:53.414 --> 00:29:58.381
Evidence export includes only the active visible issue, one hundred and one,

403
00:29:58.381 --> 00:30:03.348
with P one and its reasons. That proves all four behaviors agree.

404
00:30:03.348 --> 00:30:08.315
Rerun required checks explicitly: some UI commit and Create PR actions skip

405
00:30:08.315 --> 00:30:13.283
local Git hooks. The forty-minute orchestration lab gives you the hands-on practice.

406
00:30:13.792 --> 00:30:18.829
Keep the sample fixtures fictional and never paste credentials into task context.

407
00:30:18.829 --> 00:30:23.866
If you later connect a tool, inspect its permissions and data destination.

408
00:30:23.866 --> 00:30:28.903
Repository text and outside documents are data, not authority to ignore the

409
00:30:28.903 --> 00:30:31.001
task's rules or ownership boundary.

410
00:30:31.201 --> 00:30:36.224
Review acquired skills, plugins, and scripts before enabling them. Hooks may run

411
00:30:36.224 --> 00:30:41.246
without the model requesting the procedure. A marketplace listing is not a

412
00:30:41.246 --> 00:30:46.268
trust guarantee. Worktrees, sandboxing, and approval policies protect different things; none proves

413
00:30:46.268 --> 00:30:48.360
the generated code is correct.

414
00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:53.258
Inspect the full diff before committing. Before an external action, confirm its

415
00:30:53.258 --> 00:30:57.956
destination and required approval. Our app is local and has no write

416
00:30:57.956 --> 00:31:02.654
API, so you can practice these habits without a database or cloud

417
00:31:02.654 --> 00:31:06.569
service. Optional media regeneration is separate from the attendee labs.

418
00:31:07.083 --> 00:31:11.931
Use a direct request for a small cohesive change. Delegate substantial independent

419
00:31:11.931 --> 00:31:16.779
work with a bounded result. Repeating the same investigation in several contexts

420
00:31:16.779 --> 00:31:21.628
costs more and may not improve the answer. A tiny team with

421
00:31:21.628 --> 00:31:24.456
clear jobs often beats an impressive-looking swarm.

422
00:31:24.656 --> 00:31:29.319
Keep delegation depth bounded and include a stopping condition. Nesting has harness-specific

423
00:31:29.319 --> 00:31:33.982
support and is not a core lab requirement. Choose a model actually

424
00:31:33.982 --> 00:31:38.646
available to your account and suited to the task. Changing model and

425
00:31:38.646 --> 00:31:40.589
changing harness are different experiments.

426
00:31:40.789 --> 00:31:45.622
Measure whether the worker's result was useful and how much coordination it

427
00:31:45.622 --> 00:31:50.456
added. Lighter models may handle straightforward tasks. The app offers usage insights

428
00:31:50.456 --> 00:31:55.289
such as chronicle, but those are optional. Optimize the workflow, not the

429
00:31:55.289 --> 00:31:58.511
agent count, and respect your plan's usage allowance.

430
00:31:59.042 --> 00:32:03.875
Our issue triage board has eight fictional issues, six active and two

431
00:32:03.875 --> 00:32:08.708
closed. Two active workflows are blocked. Accessibility, payments, reporting, and search each

432
00:32:08.708 --> 00:32:13.541
contain two issues. It runs with Node's built-in HTTP server, a static

433
00:32:13.541 --> 00:32:15.957
page, and the built-in test runner.

434
00:32:16.157 --> 00:32:20.804
The starter passes its baseline checks but intentionally lacks the four lab

435
00:32:20.804 --> 00:32:25.451
enhancements. Shared wiring already follows frozen contracts, so workers can own separate

436
00:32:25.451 --> 00:32:30.098
modules and tests. Instructor answers live separately and are not hidden inside

437
00:32:30.098 --> 00:32:34.358
the normal starter. Do not copy them before trying the exercise.

438
00:32:34.558 --> 00:32:39.203
From the repository root, run npm test and npm start, then open

439
00:32:39.203 --> 00:32:43.849
the loopback page. No npm install, database, API keys, or Azure account

440
00:32:43.849 --> 00:32:48.495
is needed for the app. Use the exact paths and acceptance outputs

441
00:32:48.495 --> 00:32:53.141
in the lab files; these diagrams explain the workflow rather than replace

442
00:32:53.141 --> 00:32:54.302
the executable specification.

443
00:32:54.833 --> 00:32:59.689
You have thirty minutes. Confirm the starter and harness, then write short

444
00:32:59.689 --> 00:33:04.544
repository instructions. Create a focused custom agent with only needed tools. Create

445
00:33:04.544 --> 00:33:09.399
a matching skill directory, name, description, and supporting resource. Test them on

446
00:33:09.399 --> 00:33:11.018
a known small task.

447
00:33:11.218 --> 00:33:15.944
Show discovery separately from behavior. Inspect tool activity and compare output with

448
00:33:15.944 --> 00:33:20.671
the source. Record an explicit skill invocation and a matching plain-language request.

449
00:33:20.671 --> 00:33:25.398
Include an unavailable check reported honestly as not run. A listed file

450
00:33:25.398 --> 00:33:28.549
or a polished report is not sufficient proof.

451
00:33:28.749 --> 00:33:33.394
At the checkpoint, explain why each rule belongs in instructions, a role,

452
00:33:33.394 --> 00:33:38.040
or a skill. Use the hint ladder if you fall behind; the

453
00:33:38.040 --> 00:33:42.686
first hint is not the complete answer. The lab's measured outcome is

454
00:33:42.686 --> 00:33:46.944
a useful toolkit you can evaluate, not just more customization files.

455
00:33:47.458 --> 00:33:52.685
You have thirty-five minutes. Prepare a self-contained research brief with the contract,

456
00:33:52.685 --> 00:33:57.912
constraints, allowed actions, and expected report. Use a read-only specialist to find

457
00:33:57.912 --> 00:34:03.139
the priority behavior and existing test pattern. Review that report before implementation.

458
00:34:03.339 --> 00:34:08.066
Keep one writer for the assigned priority module and tests. Use the

459
00:34:08.066 --> 00:34:12.793
policy skill to apply the exact rules. Check positive, negative, and boundary

460
00:34:12.793 --> 00:34:17.520
cases, including closed override. A nearby example is not proof of the

461
00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:20.671
real threshold. Supply test output as execution evidence.

462
00:34:20.871 --> 00:34:25.517
Ask the restricted reviewer to inspect the requirements and changed source. It

463
00:34:25.517 --> 00:34:30.163
must not claim command execution it could not perform. If native delegation

464
00:34:30.163 --> 00:34:34.809
is unavailable, run the roles sequentially. Hand off the outcome, changed paths,

465
00:34:34.809 --> 00:34:36.357
checks, and remaining uncertainty.

466
00:34:36.875 --> 00:34:41.564
You have forty minutes. Read the shared contract and the search and

467
00:34:41.564 --> 00:34:46.252
summary tasks. Record owners, write sets, prerequisites, and reserved shared files. If

468
00:34:46.252 --> 00:34:50.941
both tasks need a contract change, complete it first. Do not send

469
00:34:50.941 --> 00:34:52.894
workers out with different assumptions.

470
00:34:53.094 --> 00:34:57.988
Compare the one-writer pattern with separate worktrees where available. Exercise claims and

471
00:34:57.988 --> 00:35:02.882
handoffs, then use the controlled scope-violation check. Explain whether it prevents a

472
00:35:02.882 --> 00:35:07.777
write or detects it later. Shared files, board updates, and Git integration

473
00:35:07.777 --> 00:35:09.408
remain with the integrator.

474
00:35:09.608 --> 00:35:14.252
Review and integrate one result at a time, then rerun the combined

475
00:35:14.252 --> 00:35:18.895
checks and try the board. Separate passing reports are not enough. At

476
00:35:18.895 --> 00:35:23.539
debrief, explain what could still collide and what you would do if

477
00:35:23.539 --> 00:35:25.860
a worker needed another owner's file.

478
00:35:26.375 --> 00:35:31.024
You have fifteen minutes. Review the complete relevant diff and requirement coverage.

479
00:35:31.024 --> 00:35:35.673
Run the final checks and user flow, then create the minimal evidence

480
00:35:35.673 --> 00:35:40.322
export. Mark unavailable checks not run with a reason. Do not turn

481
00:35:40.322 --> 00:35:42.647
missing evidence into a success statement.

482
00:35:42.847 --> 00:35:47.711
Write a handoff someone can use without reading the whole agent history.

483
00:35:47.711 --> 00:35:52.574
Include the outcome, changed paths, assumptions, exact checks and results, remaining risks,

484
00:35:52.574 --> 00:35:57.438
and next decisions. An evidence-based handoff is the final product, not an

485
00:35:57.438 --> 00:35:58.654
agent completion message.

486
00:35:58.854 --> 00:36:04.015
Ask which tasks were truly independent, whether separate contexts helped, and where

487
00:36:04.015 --> 00:36:09.175
coordination cost more than a direct edit. The live workshop ends at

488
00:36:09.175 --> 00:36:14.336
three hours. Hooks, marketplace packaging, and cloud continuity are optional extensions afterward,

489
00:36:14.336 --> 00:36:16.056
not hidden extra requirements.

490
00:36:16.583 --> 00:36:21.862
When work goes wrong, stop expanding the prompt. Inspect the actual files,

491
00:36:21.862 --> 00:36:27.140
changes, and evidence. If a customization is missing, check its path, frontmatter,

492
00:36:27.140 --> 00:36:32.419
harness, inventory, and committed worktree base. If tools differ, check the runtime

493
00:36:32.419 --> 00:36:35.058
rather than only the editor completion.

494
00:36:35.258 --> 00:36:40.259
Pause overlapping writers and choose one integration owner. Preserve useful work and

495
00:36:40.259 --> 00:36:45.259
resolve the shared contract. Do not run a destructive reset simply to

496
00:36:45.259 --> 00:36:50.260
look clean. The lab provides named backup and restore operations that preserve

497
00:36:50.260 --> 00:36:52.760
current contents before undoing specific files.

498
00:36:52.960 --> 00:36:57.669
Stopping does not undo completed commands or external actions. Checkpoints can restore

499
00:36:57.669 --> 00:37:02.378
some workspace and chat state, not every side effect. Use the appropriate

500
00:37:02.378 --> 00:37:07.086
Git or service recovery controls, then rerun affected checks. Recovery is complete

501
00:37:07.086 --> 00:37:09.833
when the required behavior is verified again.

502
00:37:10.333 --> 00:37:15.559
Your reliable teamwork recipe is a role, a reusable procedure, and a

503
00:37:15.559 --> 00:37:20.785
specific task contract. Choose narrow responsibilities and real acceptance examples. The agent

504
00:37:20.785 --> 00:37:26.010
owns a job, the skill supplies a process, and the prompt defines

505
00:37:26.010 --> 00:37:27.752
what success means today.

506
00:37:27.952 --> 00:37:33.598
Separate context does not mean separate files. Freeze contracts, assign owners, serialize

507
00:37:33.598 --> 00:37:39.243
shared changes, and use one integrator. Worktrees help separate working copies; permissions

508
00:37:39.243 --> 00:37:44.889
and sandboxing solve different problems. Advisory claims help coordination but do not

509
00:37:44.889 --> 00:37:46.300
enforce a lock.

510
00:37:46.500 --> 00:37:51.386
Evidence beats confident prose. A read-only reviewer cannot claim it ran tests.

511
00:37:51.386 --> 00:37:56.271
Target vscode does not switch the harness. Two passing branches can fail

512
00:37:56.271 --> 00:38:01.157
together, so retest after integration. Keep the smallest workflow that reliably proves

513
00:38:01.157 --> 00:38:05.228
the outcome. That's the upgrade from agent user to orchestrator.

514
00:38:05.750 --> 00:38:10.648
The full package includes the official source ledger, labs, instructor answers, captions,

515
00:38:10.648 --> 00:38:15.547
and this video's script. Product facts were checked on November seventh, twenty

516
00:38:15.547 --> 00:38:20.445
twenty-six. Recheck them before a later event. Preview features and organization policy

517
00:38:20.445 --> 00:38:23.302
can change what appears in your client.

518
00:38:23.502 --> 00:38:28.149
The animated interfaces and team diagrams are original teaching illustrations, not recordings

519
00:38:28.149 --> 00:38:32.796
of live Copilot activity. They show concepts; the labs show executable steps.

520
00:38:32.796 --> 00:38:37.443
Voice and animation are separate from the offline sample application, and they

521
00:38:37.443 --> 00:38:40.929
do not add time to the three-hour live schedule.

522
00:38:41.129 --> 00:38:45.774
Start with one small task you understand. Give it a clear brief,

523
00:38:45.774 --> 00:38:50.420
use only the help it needs, and check the result. Then add

524
00:38:50.420 --> 00:38:55.065
one specialist or one independent workstream when it genuinely helps. Keep the

525
00:38:55.065 --> 00:38:59.711
controls visible and the evidence honest. You are directing the work, not

526
00:38:59.711 --> 00:39:00.485
surrendering it.
